## Solution

**Interpreting the equation.** The series as literally written, $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}2^{n-1}(1-2d^n)$, diverges for every $d<1$ (since $2d^n\to 0$, so the summand $\to 2^{n-1}\to\infty$). The meaningful equation encoding "total removed length $=1$" (i.e. measure zero) is

$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}2^{n-1}(1-2d)^{n}=1,$$

where the exponent $n$ applies to the whole removed fraction $(1-2d)$. I solve this below.

**Setup.** In the generalized Cantor construction with retention ratio $d$ at each end, at stage $n$ there are $2^{n-1}$ intervals, each of length $d^{\,n-1}$, and from each we remove the middle portion of relative size $(1-2d)$. Hence the length removed at stage $n$ equals

$$2^{n-1}\,d^{\,n-1}\,(1-2d).$$

The Cantor set has Lebesgue measure zero precisely when the total removed length equals $1$ (the full initial interval). Equivalently, the surviving length after $n$ stages is $(2d)^n$, and $\lim_{n\to\infty}(2d)^n=0$ iff $2d<1$.

**Solving the equation.** The total removed length is

$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}2^{n-1}(1-2d)^{n}
=(1-2d)\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\bigl[2(1-2d)\bigr]^{n}
=\frac{1-2d}{1-2(1-2d)}
=\frac{1-2d}{4d-1},$$

valid when $|2(1-2d)|<1$, i.e. $\tfrac14<d<\tfrac34$. Setting this equal to $1$:

$$\frac{1-2d}{4d-1}=1\;\Longrightarrow\;1-2d=4d-1\;\Longrightarrow\;2=6d\;\Longrightarrow\;d=\frac13.$$

**Verification.** For $d=\tfrac13$: $2d=\tfrac23<1$, so $\lim(2d)^n=0$ and the set has measure zero. ✓ The removed fraction per stage is $1-2d=\tfrac13$, recovering the classical middle-thirds Cantor set. The convergence condition $\tfrac14<d<\tfrac34$ is satisfied. ✓

$$\boxed{d=\dfrac13}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
